"154.2\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#ffffff\"/>\n      <circle cx=\"204.4\" cy=\"160.2\" r=\"1.1\" fill=\"#ffffff\" opacity=\"0.85\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 196 144 Q 203 141 211 145\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ffbd7a\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" opacity=\"0.75\"/>\n    </g>\n\n    <path d=\"M 169 184 Q 178 180 187 184 Q 186 192 178 194 Q 170 192 169 184 Z\" fill=\"#1c1412\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"176\" cy=\"185.5\" rx=\"3.2\" ry=\"1.5\" fill=\"#5e4b46\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 178 194 L 178 197 M 171 196 Q 174.5 199.5 178 197 Q 181.5 199.5 185 196\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#36241e\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  </g>\n\n  <g fill=\"#2b5752\">\n    <path d=\"M 48 345 Q 42 325 32 316 Q 46 322 54 345 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 55 347 Q 58 322 68 310 Q 62 328 62 347 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 62 348 Q 70 330 82 324 Q 72 336 68 348 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 320 344 Q 326 324 338 314 Q 332 330 327 344 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 332 346 Q 344 328 358 320 Q 346 336 338 346 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 314 344 Q 308 328 298 322 Q 308 334 310 344 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n\n  <g fill=\"#ffe885\">\n    <circle cx=\"96\" cy=\"252\" r=\"2.2\" opacity=\"0.85\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"96\" cy=\"252\" r=\"6\" opacity=\"0.22\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"272\" cy=\"158\" r=\"1.8\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"272\" cy=\"158\" r=\"5\" opacity=\"0.2\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"118\" cy=\"298\" r=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0.7\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"118\" cy=\"298\" r=\"4.5\" opacity=\"0.18\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"356\" cy=\"242\" r=\"1.8\" opacity=\"0.75\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"356\" cy=\"242\" r=\"5\" opacity=\"0.18\"/>\n  </g>\n</svg>", "razonamiento_pedido": "no", "razonamiento": "**SVG Fox Creation: A Detailed Plan**\n\nAlright, let's break this down. The user wants an SVG of a stylized fox. No comments, under 8000 characters, square canvas, no external resources, got it. My task is to produce a high-quality, aesthetically pleasing fox illustration. I'm envisioning a detailed fox, rendered in SVG code, sitting on a small grassy mound against a subtle night/twilight backdrop, emphasizing volume through gradients and well-defined shapes.\n\nMy strategy involves constructing the illustration in layers. First, a gradient background, maybe a circular element to mimic the moon, possibly a dark forest environment. I'm leaning toward a deep forest green background and a creamy moon to really make the fox's orange pop. The core of the fox's design will have him sitting, slightly angled, with a large, bushy tail curled around his front paws.\n\nThe fox's construction is key. I'm planning it out in layers. Starting with the background: a gradient sky, maybe a moon, maybe some subtle stars and soft mountain silhouettes, and the ground. Then, I'll define the fox's rear tail, orange with a fluffy white tip and shadows. The body follows, with an orange gradient, and a fluffy white chest. Then, the front paws—orange tops, dark brown/black \"socks,\" and some subtle claws. Then the head—a soft triangular shape, white cheeks, large ears with pink/cream interiors and dark edges. Lastly, facial details: expressive eyes with highlights, a dark snout with shine, and subtle whiskers. Finally, I'll add some ambient details: grass blades, leaves, and maybe some strategically placed shadows.\n\nI'm keeping the coordinates system in mind, mapping out: The horizontal center will be at x=200, the ground line around y=310-400. For the moon, a circle at cx=290, cy=105, r=42. The fox’s shadow, an ellipse (200, 338), rx=95, ry=16. I'll place the body between x=145 and x=255, y=185 to 335. The white chest is y=195 to 305. Front paws: left: x=178-196, right: x=206-224 ending at y=338. The head's center at x=195, y=155. It'll range from 130 to 260 wide (including fluffy cheeks) and 105 to 205 high. Ears: Left at (125, 65), Right at (265, 65). Now, the tail: out from the right/back of the body around (x=240, y=300), curving around the right up to (315, 225) or wrapping towards the left. I am leaning toward the wrapping concept for a balanced cute feeling.\n\nThe final coordinates adjustment for balance: Head at x=175, y=155. Body spans x=130 to x=235, y=190 to 335. Front paws: x=162 and x=194, down to y=338. The folded rear leg is just right of the body around (220, 290). The tail now emanates from (225, 315), curling right-down (295, 335), up to (335, 270) and ending as a curled white tip to (295, 210).\n\nCharacter count? Should be easy. 8000 characters is plenty for a clean SVG with gradients, smooth filters (maybe), and lots of details. I'm aiming for 4500-6500 to be safe. Every shape will be constructed with mathematical precision, making sure all curves use smooth `C` and `Q` commands.\n\nColor-wise, I'm thinking: The sky will be deep blues, the moon creamy. For hills and ground, I'll use darker bluish-green, and the fox will be a striking orange with light highlights. I'll use a white/cream for chest, cheeks, and tail tip and dark brown/black for paws, ear tips, and nose. Interior ears I'll make a subtle light pink. For the eyes: Black with white highlights and a touch of amber.\n\nI'll verify all the curves step by step, the ears, head, body, paws, tail, etc. For each element, I've got", "motivo_fin": "stop", "tokens_entrada": 122, "tokens_salida": 6234, "tokens_cache_leidos": null, "tokens_cache_escritos": null, "latencia_s": 86.83, "intentos": 1, "error": null}
